WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.790
Now, let's go a bit deeper, because

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.670
contrary to popular belief, a skill is

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.790
actually not just a markdown file,

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.110
because it can expand beyond that. And

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.830
yes, you may have skills that are really

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.470
simple. I've seen skills that are only

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.470
like two three lines long, and they are

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.590
working quite effectively. But for your

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.710
most complex tasks, you can actually

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.790
have a skill that is not only composed

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.870
of just one markdown file. To give one

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:25.030
example, there is a skill that I use

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:26.910
consistently called {slash} last 30

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.470
days. And what it does is extract

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.910
information on the past 30 days across

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.030
eight different sources. And so, you can

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:35.990
imagine that this skill may be a bit

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.830
more complex than just a text file. And

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.310
so, if we navigate to that skills folder

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.110
in our workspace, you can see that last

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.510
30 days, not only does it have this

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.790
markdown file that provides instructions

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.390
or the recipe card for the agent, but it

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.870
also has scripts or code in here that

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.070
allows it to scrape sources for us. It

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.430
has references of what the brief should

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.030
look like, and a couple of other files

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.830
that help it achieve its objective. And

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.430
now, this is actually a useful skill, so

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.310
I'll teach you in the succeeding module

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.990
on how you can install it safely and

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.790
securely, especially since you may be

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.630
finding skills that you want to install

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.870
from online into your own workspace. But

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.790
for now, what's important to realize is

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.510
that when you create skills, it's

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.710
possible that it is much richer than

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.230
just a markdown file. To give you one

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.110
example from my workspace, again, the

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:21.790
Robo skill, that is a pretty

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.790
well-defined skill already, but under

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.190
that folder, you can see that there's

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.270
this brandbook.html.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.550
So, if I open this and just open that in

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.590
the browser, you can see that this is

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.790
going to be a reference brandbook for us

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.110
on the style of slides and web design

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.390
that we have for this specific skill.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.550
And so, my advice to you is to just let

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.230
these assets and artifacts naturally

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.670
come about. If in the process of

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.630
building this skill, Code Suggest to

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.550
create artifacts as part of that skill,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.070
then you can just direct it to that as

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.030
well. Because with all of these skills,

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.550
you really don't need to read them top

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.750
to bottom. For example, when I was

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.590
creating this design system, it just

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.630
became natural for us to create a brand

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.110
book such as the one that you saw

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.030
earlier. And when I ask Claude code to

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.910
update this skill, it just added this

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.030
line where that brand book is invoked so

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.190
that that would be a good reference for

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.430
the visual style for whenever this skill

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.870
is used. Now, as a direct result of that

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:14.630
realization, you can also start to think

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:16.710
about the fact that skills can actually

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.270
call other skills. And just to

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.070
illustrate, let's say we want to tweak

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.190
the Robo skill so that whenever I invoke

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.110
it, I always want Claude code to use the

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.030
align skill so that it asks me

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.710
clarifying questions, which may be

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:29.990
important because with a lot of these

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.590
designs and slides, they may be token

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:33.350
intensive, right? So, it would be in our

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:35.110
best interest to first be aligned with

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.150
our agent before it does the work for

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.949
us. And so, if you send that prompt,

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:40.510
what it will basically do is think

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:41.990
through the problem and edit the

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.910
relevant skill, in this case this Robo

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.830
skill.md, and add that line. Now, with

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:48.670
Claude code, if you are not a developer,

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.990
this may intimidate you to see a lot of

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:52.510
the edits happening in real time, but

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.470
just think of your agent as an employee

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:56.710
that is more verbose than you would like

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.230
it to be. Because what it's literally

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.990
doing here is it's thinking through the

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.790
problem and actually writing out those

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.110
steps for you. But you as the manager in

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.310
this case, you don't necessarily need to

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:07.870
go through all of the thoughts of your

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.270
employee, right? You just out of the

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:10.870
problem and you're only concerned with

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.830
the final output that they provide to

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.470
you. And so, you can see here that it's

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.590
done. It changed the skill so that it

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.390
added a step zero. So, now if you go

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.790
back to that Robo skill, you can see

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.150
what was added here is this step zero

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.990
where before any Robo work, always

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.910
invoke the align skill directly. And so,

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.270
what will happen there is it'll just

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:28.990
naturally flow so that whenever it

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.150
invokes this skill, it gets this prompt

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.070
essentially in that session and it will

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:35.190
go and find the align skill to read that

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.720
as well.
